You are working on the ExamFlow project.

We are now starting:

PHASE 4 — ROOM & SEAT MANAGEMENT

The goal of this phase is to implement the physical examination location
infrastructure used by ExamFlow.

This phase establishes:

- Buildings
- Rooms
- Room capacity
- Seat layouts
- Individual seats
- Seat availability
- Room selection for exams

This phase provides the infrastructure required for the next phase:

PHASE 5 — SEATING ASSIGNMENT

IMPORTANT:

This phase must NOT assign students to seats.

Do NOT implement automatic seating assignment.

Do NOT implement seating algorithms.

Do NOT generate seating maps yet.

Do NOT generate signature lists yet.

Do NOT generate personalized exam PDFs yet.

Do NOT integrate Crowdmark yet.

==================================================
1. READ PROJECT DOCUMENTATION FIRST
==================================================

Before coding, read:

- doc/prd.md
- doc/Architecture.md
- doc/DATABASE.md
- doc/SECURITY.md
- doc/DEVELOPMENT.md
- doc/AGENTS.md

Also inspect:

- Phase 1 Authentication
- Phase 2 Course + Exam Management
- Phase 3 Student + Roster Management

Follow existing project conventions.

Do not introduce a new architecture.

First provide a concise implementation plan.

Then implement the plan.

==================================================
2. PHASE 4 SCOPE
==================================================

Implement:

1. Building management foundation
2. Room management
3. Room capacity
4. Seat layout
5. Seat records
6. Seat availability
7. Room selection for an exam
8. Room validation
9. Seat generation
10. Room/seat management UI
11. Authorization
12. Audit events
13. Database migrations
14. Backend APIs
15. Frontend UI
16. Automated tests

Do NOT implement:

- Student-to-seat assignment
- Automatic seating algorithm
- Random seating
- Alphabetical seating
- Exam printing
- PDF generation
- Crowdmark integration
- QR codes
- Seating maps
- Signature lists

==================================================
3. CORE DESIGN PRINCIPLE
==================================================

Keep the physical location model simple.

The system should distinguish:

Building
    ↓
Room
    ↓
Seat

and separately:

Exam
    ↓
Exam Room

and later:

Exam
    ↓
Student
    ↓
Seat Assignment

Do not combine these concepts.

==================================================
4. BUILDING MODEL
==================================================

Create a Building entity if not already defined.

Suggested fields:

id
building_code
building_name
created_at
updated_at

Examples:

IB
Instructional Centre
HW
Health Sciences Building

Do not hard-code building names.

==================================================
5. BUILDING CODE
==================================================

building_code must be:

- required
- unique
- normalized
- reasonably length-limited

Do not assume a specific university building-code format.

Do not use building name as the unique identifier.

==================================================
6. ROOM MODEL
==================================================

Create a Room entity.

Suggested fields:

id
building_id
room_number
room_name
capacity
is_active
created_at
updated_at

Use only fields required by the product requirements.

If room_name is unnecessary, do not create it.

==================================================
7. ROOM IDENTITY
==================================================

A room should be uniquely identified by:

building + room_number

For example:

IB 101

and:

HW 101

are different rooms.

The database should enforce appropriate uniqueness.

Do not make room_number globally unique.

==================================================
8. ROOM CAPACITY
==================================================

Room capacity represents the maximum number of usable examination seats.

It should be:

- positive
- reasonable
- validated by the backend

Do not assume room capacity is simply the number of physical seats.

Some physical seats may be unavailable.

Therefore:

room.capacity

and:

usable seats

should be treated as related but distinct concepts.

==================================================
9. ACTIVE / INACTIVE ROOMS
==================================================

Rooms should support:

active
inactive

An inactive room should not be selectable for a new exam unless explicitly
authorized.

Do not delete rooms simply because they are no longer available.

Historical exam records may depend on them.

==================================================
10. ROOM DESCRIPTION
==================================================

Do not create unnecessary facilities-management fields.

Do NOT add:

- projector
- windows
- floor
- temperature
- accessibility equipment
- maintenance schedule

unless explicitly required by doc/prd.md.

This is an exam administration system, not a facilities management system.

==================================================
11. SEAT MODEL
==================================================

Create a Seat entity.

Suggested fields:

id
room_id
seat_label
row_label
seat_number
is_usable
created_at
updated_at

Follow the actual architecture and requirements.

The exact representation may be simplified if a better design exists.

==================================================
12. SEAT IDENTITY
==================================================

A seat must be uniquely identifiable within a room.

For example:

IB 101
A01

IB 101
A02

IB 101
A03

The same seat label may exist in another room.

Therefore:

unique(room_id, seat_label)

should be enforced.

==================================================
13. SEAT LABELS
==================================================

Seat labels should be human-readable.

Examples:

A01
A02
A03

or:

R01-C01
R01-C02

Do not force a single physical layout format if the requirements do not
require one.

Keep the data model flexible enough to represent normal university
classroom seating.

==================================================
14. ROW AND POSITION

If row/position information is stored, treat it as physical metadata.

For example:

row_label = A
seat_number = 1

The system should be able to sort seats predictably.

This will be important later for:

- seating maps
- signature lists
- deterministic student assignment

Do not implement those features yet.

==================================================
15. SEAT USABILITY
==================================================

A seat should support at least:

usable
unusable

An unusable seat should not be available for exam seating assignment.

Examples:

- broken chair
- blocked desk
- instructor station
- equipment location
- physical obstruction

Do not delete unusable seats.

Historical assignments may depend on them.

==================================================
16. SEAT STATUS

Keep seat status simple.

Do NOT create a complicated state machine.

For example:

is_usable = true/false

is sufficient unless doc/prd.md requires more.

Do not create:

AVAILABLE
RESERVED
OCCUPIED
BLOCKED
MAINTENANCE
CLEANING
etc.

Seat occupancy belongs to an exam assignment, not to the physical seat itself.

==================================================
17. IMPORTANT SEPARATION

A physical seat is NOT an exam seat assignment.

Physical:

Room IB 101
Seat A01

Exam-specific:

Exam CSC108 Final
Student 100000001
Seat IB101-A01

The second relationship belongs to Phase 5.

Do not put:

student_id

inside the Seat table.

==================================================
18. SEAT GENERATION

Implement a simple mechanism to create seats for a room.

Example:

Rows:
A
B
C
D

Seats per row:
10

Generate:

A01
A02
...
A10

B01
B02
...
B10

etc.

The exact UI may vary.

==================================================
19. SEAT GENERATION SAFETY

Generating seats must not blindly delete existing seats.

If seats already exist:

- detect them
- avoid duplicates
- report conflicts

Do not destroy physical layout data automatically.

If regeneration is required, it must be explicit and carefully validated.

==================================================
20. ROOM CAPACITY VS SEAT COUNT

Do not assume:

capacity == number of seats

Instead:

room.capacity
    =
maximum permitted exam capacity

usable seat count
    =
number of usable seats currently available

For example:

Room capacity = 200

Physical seats = 200

Usable seats = 185

The exam seating process should later use 185, not 200.

==================================================
21. ROOM API

Implement only APIs needed by this phase.

Possible endpoints:

GET    /api/buildings
POST   /api/buildings
PATCH  /api/buildings/{id}

GET    /api/rooms
POST   /api/rooms
GET    /api/rooms/{id}
PATCH  /api/rooms/{id}

GET    /api/rooms/{id}/seats
POST   /api/rooms/{id}/seats
PATCH  /api/rooms/{id}/seats/{seat_id}

Add DELETE only if explicitly required.

Prefer deactivation over destructive deletion.

==================================================
22. SEAT GENERATION API

If seat generation is implemented as a backend operation:

POST:

/api/rooms/{room_id}/seats/generate

The request may contain:

row_count
seats_per_row

or an equivalent simple structure.

The backend must validate all parameters.

Do not allow arbitrary database manipulation through generation parameters.

==================================================
23. EXAM ROOM RELATIONSHIP

An exam must be able to have one or more rooms.

Create an appropriate relationship entity.

Suggested:

exam_rooms

Fields may include:

id
exam_id
room_id
created_at
updated_at

Do not yet include:

student_id
seat_id

Those belong to Phase 5.

==================================================
24. EXAM ROOM UNIQUENESS

The same room should not be added twice to the same exam.

Database constraint:

unique(exam_id, room_id)

must be enforced.

==================================================
25. EXAM ROOM CAPACITY

The system should be able to calculate:

Total exam capacity

based on selected rooms and usable seats.

For example:

Exam:

CSC108 Final

Rooms:

IB101 → 80 usable seats
IB102 → 70 usable seats

Total usable capacity:

150

This value is informational in Phase 4.

Do not assign students yet.

==================================================
26. ROOM SELECTION FOR EXAMS

Authorized users should be able to add rooms to an exam.

Example:

Exam
CSC108 Final

Selected rooms:

IB101
IB102
IB103

Display:

Total available seats:
230

Do not create seating assignments.

==================================================
27. EXAM ROOM REMOVAL

Authorized users should be able to remove a room from an exam if there are
no later-phase dependencies preventing it.

However:

Do not allow removing a room in a way that would silently invalidate existing
seating assignments once Phase 5 exists.

Since Phase 5 does not yet exist, implement the basic relationship safely.

Document future constraints.

==================================================
28. ROOM AVAILABILITY

Phase 4 should provide a foundation for determining whether a room can be
used for an exam.

If doc/prd.md explicitly requires scheduling conflict detection, implement
basic date/time conflict checking.

Otherwise:

Do NOT build a full room scheduling engine.

At minimum, do not allow obviously duplicate room assignments to the same
exam.

Document any deferred scheduling functionality.

==================================================
29. ROOM CONFLICT CHECKING

If conflict checking is implemented:

Compare:

exam date
exam start time
exam end time

against other exams using the same room.

Overlapping intervals should be detected.

Example:

Exam A:
10:00–12:00

Exam B:
11:00–13:00

Conflict.

Exam B:
12:00–14:00

No overlap if end time is exclusive.

Be explicit and consistent about interval semantics.

==================================================
30. TIMEZONE

Use the same application/institutional timezone strategy established in
previous phases.

Do not create room-specific timezone logic.

==================================================
31. AUTHORIZATION

Use Phase 1 authorization.

Suggested:

ADMIN:
- manage buildings
- manage rooms
- manage seats
- assign rooms to exams

STAFF:
- manage rooms and exam rooms if authorized

INSTRUCTOR:
- view room information for authorized exams
- should not modify room infrastructure unless explicitly authorized

Do not invent complex permissions.

Backend enforcement is mandatory.

==================================================
32. IDOR PROTECTION

Protect endpoints such as:

GET /api/rooms/{id}

GET /api/exams/{exam_id}/rooms

PATCH /api/rooms/{id}

POST /api/exams/{exam_id}/rooms

The existence of a UUID must not automatically grant access.

Verify authorization and resource relationships.

==================================================
33. FRONTEND ROUTES

Create simple administrative pages.

Suggested:

/app/buildings
/app/rooms
/app/rooms/[id]

and:

/app/exams/[id]/rooms

Follow existing application structure.

==================================================
34. BUILDING UI

Provide:

Building list
Create building
Edit building

Keep it simple.

Do not build a facilities-management dashboard.

==================================================
35. ROOM LIST UI

Display:

Building
Room Number
Capacity
Usable Seats
Status

Provide:

- search
- filtering
- pagination

Do not load all rooms if the institution has many rooms.

==================================================
36. ROOM DETAIL UI

Display:

Building
Room number
Capacity
Active/inactive
Seat count
Usable seat count

Provide:

- edit room
- manage seats
- generate seats if authorized

==================================================
37. SEAT MANAGEMENT UI

Display a clear seat table.

Example:

------------------------------------------------
Room IB 101

Row    Seat    Status
A      A01     Usable
A      A02     Usable
A      A03     Unusable
B      B01     Usable
------------------------------------------------

Provide simple filtering.

Do not create a visual seating map yet.

A table is sufficient in Phase 4.

==================================================
38. SEAT GENERATION UI

Provide a simple form:

Number of rows:
[ 10 ]

Seats per row:
[ 12 ]

[ Generate Seats ]

Before execution, display a confirmation.

For example:

"This will create 120 physical seats."

Do not silently overwrite existing seats.

==================================================
39. EXAM ROOM UI

On the exam detail page provide:

Rooms

[ Add Room ]

Display:

Building
Room
Capacity
Usable Seats

Total:

XXX usable seats

Do not show student seat assignments yet.

==================================================
40. VALIDATION

Backend validation must enforce:

Building:

- building_code required
- unique
- reasonable length

Room:

- building exists
- room number required
- capacity > 0

Seat:

- room exists
- seat label required
- unique within room

Exam Room:

- exam exists
- room exists
- no duplicate room relationship

==================================================
41. DATABASE CONSTRAINTS

Use database constraints.

At minimum:

Building:

unique(building_code)

Room:

unique(building_id, room_number)

Seat:

unique(room_id, seat_label)

ExamRoom:

unique(exam_id, room_id)

Use foreign keys correctly.

==================================================
42. DATABASE MIGRATION

Create new Alembic migration(s).

Create:

buildings
rooms
seats
exam_rooms

Add:

- primary keys
- foreign keys
- indexes
- unique constraints

Do not modify historical migrations unnecessarily.

Verify on a fresh database.

==================================================
43. INDEXES

Add indexes based on actual query patterns.

Likely:

buildings.building_code
rooms.building_id
rooms.room_number
seats.room_id
exam_rooms.exam_id
exam_rooms.room_id

Do not add unnecessary indexes.

==================================================
44. SERVICE LAYER

Follow the existing architecture:

API
 ↓
Service
 ↓
Database

Keep room/seat business logic in services.

Do not create a generic "location engine."

==================================================
45. API RESPONSE MODELS

Use Pydantic models.

Examples:

BuildingResponse
RoomResponse
SeatResponse
ExamRoomResponse

Do not return raw ORM objects.

==================================================
46. PAGINATION

Use pagination for:

- buildings if needed
- rooms
- seats where appropriate
- exam room lists if large

Do not load an enormous number of records into the browser.

==================================================
47. SEARCH

Provide basic search:

Rooms:

- building code
- building name
- room number

Do not create full-text search infrastructure.

==================================================
48. ROOM CAPACITY CALCULATION

The backend should calculate usable seat count.

Do not trust a frontend-provided number.

Example:

usable_seats =
COUNT(seats WHERE is_usable = true)

If no physical seat records have been generated yet, return an appropriate
value and state.

Do not silently assume every capacity seat exists.

==================================================
49. ROOM CAPACITY VALIDATION

If:

usable seats > room.capacity

the system should flag this as inconsistent.

Do not automatically delete seats.

Provide a clear validation error or warning according to the product design.

==================================================
50. EXAM CAPACITY

For an exam:

total_usable_capacity =
sum(usable seats in selected rooms)

Display this value.

Do not yet compare it against student roster size unless Phase 3 data is
already available and the product requires the warning.

If you implement the comparison, make it informational only.

Example:

Students registered: 180
Usable seats: 160

Warning:

"Selected rooms do not currently provide enough usable seats."

Do NOT automatically change rooms.

==================================================
51. NO AUTOMATIC ASSIGNMENT

This is critical.

Do NOT implement:

student → room
student → seat
random seat
alphabetical seat
alternating seat
spacing algorithm

Those belong to Phase 5.

==================================================
52. NO SEATING MAP

Do not generate a graphical seating map.

The seat table is enough.

Phase 8 will generate the instructor-facing seating map.

==================================================
53. NO SIGNATURE LIST

Do not generate signature lists.

Phase 8 will generate them after seating assignments exist.

==================================================
54. NO CROWDMARK

Do not integrate Crowdmark.

Do not create:

crowdmark_id
crowdmark_api_key
crowdmark_template
crowdmark_document

Those belong to later phases.

==================================================
55. NO PDF GENERATION

Do not generate personalized exams.

Do not create PDF files.

Do not create document templates.

==================================================
56. NO AI

Do not introduce:

OpenAI
LLM
RAG
LangChain
LlamaIndex
embeddings
machine learning

Room and seat management is deterministic.

==================================================
57. AUDIT EVENTS

Add audit events for important administrative actions.

Examples:

BUILDING_CREATED
BUILDING_UPDATED

ROOM_CREATED
ROOM_UPDATED
ROOM_DEACTIVATED

SEAT_GENERATED
SEAT_UPDATED

EXAM_ROOM_ADDED
EXAM_ROOM_REMOVED

Do not log every read operation.

Do not log unnecessary sensitive information.

==================================================
58. AUDIT DATA

Audit records should contain:

actor
action
resource type
resource ID
timestamp
safe summary

Do not log:

- passwords
- tokens
- secrets
- student PII

Student data should not be included in Phase 4 audit events unless absolutely
necessary.

==================================================
59. TESTING — BUILDINGS

Test:

1. Create building
2. Duplicate building code rejected
3. Retrieve building
4. Update building
5. Authorization
6. Inactive/deactivated behavior

==================================================
60. TESTING — ROOMS

Test:

1. Create room
2. Duplicate room within same building rejected
3. Same room number in different buildings allowed
4. Invalid capacity rejected
5. Retrieve room
6. Update room
7. Inactive room behavior
8. Authorization

==================================================
61. TESTING — SEATS

Test:

1. Create seat
2. Duplicate seat label within room rejected
3. Same seat label in different rooms allowed
4. Mark seat unusable
5. Seat count
6. Usable seat count
7. Seat generation
8. Seat generation does not create duplicates

==================================================
62. TESTING — EXAM ROOMS

Test:

1. Add room to exam
2. Duplicate room rejected
3. Remove room
4. Calculate total usable capacity
5. Unauthorized modification rejected
6. Invalid exam rejected
7. Invalid room rejected

==================================================
63. TESTING — IDOR

Explicitly test that a user cannot:

- access unauthorized room
- modify unauthorized room
- modify another exam's room assignments

Changing a UUID must not bypass authorization.

==================================================
64. TESTING — SEAT GENERATION

Test:

Example:

5 rows
10 seats per row

Expected:

50 seats.

Also test:

- invalid row count
- invalid seats per row
- duplicate generation
- transaction rollback
- existing seats are preserved

==================================================
65. FRONTEND TESTING

Test:

- building list
- room list
- room detail
- seat list
- seat generation
- exam room selection
- capacity display
- validation errors
- authorization behavior

Do not create excessive UI tests.

==================================================
66. ACCESSIBILITY

Follow existing accessibility standards.

Ensure:

- form labels
- keyboard navigation
- semantic tables
- accessible buttons
- confirmation dialogs
- readable validation errors
- focus management

==================================================
67. DEVELOPMENT SEED DATA

If seed data is useful, create synthetic development data.

Example:

Buildings:

TEST
Test Building

Rooms:

TEST 101
TEST 102

Generate synthetic seats.

Never use actual university room data unless it has been explicitly provided
as development-safe data.

==================================================
68. PRODUCTION DATA

Do not commit real:

- room lists
- building lists
- student information
- exam information

to Git.

Production institutional data must be imported or configured separately.

==================================================
69. DOCUMENTATION

Update:

README.md
doc/DATABASE.md
doc/DEVELOPMENT.md

Update doc/Architecture.md if necessary.

Document:

- building model
- room model
- seat model
- exam-room relationship
- seat generation
- development seed data
- API endpoints

Do not document future functionality as if it already exists.

==================================================
70. DEPENDENCIES

Do not add unnecessary dependencies.

Do not introduce:

Redis
RabbitMQ
Kafka
Elasticsearch
Kubernetes
microservices

A PostgreSQL-backed implementation is sufficient.

==================================================
71. PERFORMANCE

The system should support normal university-scale room inventories.

Use:

- pagination
- indexes
- efficient queries

Avoid:

- loading all seats unnecessarily
- N+1 queries
- recalculating large datasets repeatedly

Do not introduce caching prematurely.

==================================================
72. CONCURRENCY

Database constraints must protect against duplicate:

- buildings
- rooms
- seats
- exam-room relationships

Handle concurrent conflicts gracefully.

Do not introduce distributed locking.

==================================================
73. DATA INTEGRITY

Do not allow:

Room without Building

Seat without Room

ExamRoom without Exam

ExamRoom without Room

Database foreign keys must enforce these relationships.

==================================================
74. HISTORICAL DATA

Do not physically delete buildings, rooms, or seats if doing so could break
historical exam information.

Prefer:

is_active

or:

is_usable

where appropriate.

Historical records must remain understandable.

==================================================
75. IMPORTANT FUTURE COMPATIBILITY

Phase 4 must provide clean inputs for Phase 5.

Phase 5 will need to query:

Exam
  ↓
Selected Rooms
  ↓
Usable Seats
  ↓
Students
  ↓
Seat Assignments

Therefore:

- seat identity must be stable
- room identity must be stable
- seat ordering must be deterministic
- usable seats must be queryable
- exam-room relationships must be explicit

Do not implement the assignment algorithm yet.

==================================================
76. SEAT ORDERING

Define a deterministic ordering.

For example:

row_label ASC
seat_number ASC

or:

seat_label ASC

The exact strategy should be documented.

This ordering will later be used by seating assignment.

Do not depend on PostgreSQL's natural row order.

==================================================
77. API DOCUMENTATION

Document all new endpoints through FastAPI/OpenAPI.

Use:

- request models
- response models
- clear status codes
- authorization requirements

Do not expose internal database details unnecessarily.

==================================================
78. ERROR HANDLING

Use:

401:
unauthenticated

403:
unauthorized

404:
resource not found

409:
duplicate/conflict

422:
validation error

Do not expose stack traces.

==================================================
79. MANUAL VALIDATION

Perform:

1. Login as ADMIN.
2. Create building.
3. Create room.
4. Set room capacity.
5. Generate seats.
6. Mark one seat unusable.
7. Verify usable seat count.
